  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🌿 Therapy Practice Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Calm, trustworthy names for a private therapy, counselling or psychotherapy practice."
    },
    "intro": "Good therapy practice names feel calm, safe and professional: a nature word (<em>Cedar Counselling Rooms</em>), your own name, or a gentle phrase (<em>Space to Talk</em>). A client often finds a therapist on a hard day, so the name should reassure rather than impress. This **Therapy Practice Name Generator** suggests names for therapy, counselling and psychotherapy practices with US or UK wording (counseling or counselling), including names built from your town or your own name.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good therapy practice names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Cedar Counselling Rooms</strong>: calm and natural, with UK spelling.</li><li><strong>Still Meadow Therapy Rooms</strong>: quiet and spacious, which is what clients want to feel.</li><li><strong>Fernbrook Psychotherapy & Counselling</strong>: says both services plainly.</li><li><strong>The Ashworth Practice</strong>: a classic name that could grow into a group practice.</li><li><strong>Space to Talk Behavioral Health</strong>: a warm phrase with US wording.</li><li><strong>Turning Leaf Counseling Group</strong>: hopeful without promising too much.</li><li><strong>Cairn Family Therapy</strong>: a modern one-word name; a cairn marks the path for walkers.</li><li><strong>Steady Therapy Collective</strong>: short and reassuring.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Words, titles and what clients search for",
        "content": "<p>Clients usually search for the service, so say it: <em>counselling</em>, <em>psychotherapy</em>, <em>family therapy</em>. Choose a region for the right spelling: <em>counseling</em> and <em>behavioral health</em> in the US, <em>counselling</em> and <em>wellbeing</em> in the UK.</p><p>Be careful with professional titles. In the UK, \"practitioner psychologist\" titles such as <em>clinical psychologist</em> and <em>counselling psychologist</em> are protected, and only people registered with the Health and Care Professions Council (HCPC) can use them. <em>Counsellor</em> and <em>psychotherapist</em> aren't protected by law, but clients often look for membership of a professional body such as the BACP or UKCP. In the US, titles like <em>Licensed Professional Counselor</em> are set by each state's licensing board.</p><p>The calm style draws on trees, water and open space; the warm style on phrases about talking and finding your way. Avoid names that promise a cure or sound clinical when you aren't a clinic.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>Press <strong>Check</strong> on any name, or use the <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a>, to see whether it's free as a domain and a social handle and to search the company register. Before you commit:</p><ul><li><strong>Company register:</strong> Companies House in the UK, or your state's business register in the US.</li><li><strong>Trade marks:</strong> search the UK IPO or USPTO register for anyone using the name in your field.</li><li><strong>Locally:</strong> search Google Maps for your area, since parents and clients will find you there first.</li><li><strong>Your professional register:</strong> make sure the practice name sits comfortably with the title on your registration or membership, so clients can check you easily.</li></ul><p>For the full process, see <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a>. This is general guidance, not legal advice.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Choosing between your name and a place name",
        "content": "<p>A solo practitioner often uses their own name, because clients are choosing a person. A place or nature name is better if you plan to add colleagues or rent rooms to other therapists. For other care businesses, try the <a href=\"home-care-name-generator.html\">Home Care Name Generator</a>.</p><p>For more, see all the <a href=\"../categories/biz_care-names.html\">pets, care and learning business name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What are good therapy practice names?",
        "answer": "Calm, professional names: a nature word like Cedar Counselling Rooms, a gentle phrase like Space to Talk, or your own name plus the service. Choose US or UK wording for counseling or counselling."
      },
      {
        "question": "Should I use my own name for my therapy practice?",
        "answer": "Many therapists in solo practice do, because clients are choosing a person: <em>Counselling with Sarah</em> or your full name plus <em>Psychotherapy</em> feels personal and is easy to check against your professional registration. A place or nature name (<em>Willow Tree Counseling</em>) suits you better if you plan to add colleagues or sell the practice one day. In the UK, avoid protected titles such as <em>clinical psychologist</em> or <em>counselling psychologist</em> unless you're registered with the HCPC."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I check a therapy practice name is free?",
        "answer": "Press **Check** on any name to see whether it's free as a company name, a domain and a social media handle. Before you commit, search the official registers too: in the UK, Companies House; in the US, your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I call my practice a psychology practice?",
        "answer": "Only if it fits your registration. In the UK the protected titles are the practitioner psychologist titles, such as clinical psychologist, which need HCPC registration; in the US, rules on psychology titles are set by each state. If you're a counsellor or psychotherapist, say that instead."
      }
    ]
  },
